# ISSUES

## REMARK
Taskgrind currently has a work around to all these issues :
    - the `--outermost-only` CLA makes only tasks outermost-scope instrumented
But it looses all the point of Valgrind ("seeing everything" goes to "seeing only outermost scope")
Another workaround could be to ask the programmer for a symbol whitelist
    - "omp_task_entry"
    - "gemm"
    - "a_suspicious_library_function"
    - [...]


## CAN CAUSE FALSE-NEGATIVE

### LLVM task serialization
#### Problem
https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/89398#issuecomment-2066822457
#### How Taskgrind manages it (currently, fixing LLVM as suggested would require no such work-around)
Run with 2 threads, and disable throttling



## CAN CAUSE FALSE-POSITIVE

### Atomic Accesses
- may or may not be an issue if order matters
- no support in taskgrind



### Any synchronization primitives not reported to Taskgrind
- Mutexes, conditions, ... - NO SUPPORT IN TASKGRIND YET
    - we could detect those using `omp_lock_t` via OMPT callbacks
    - we could detect pthread matching symbols name
- Spinlock
    - i don't think we can detect ?
#### How Taskgrind manages it
It doesn't



### Malloc recycling
#### Problem
- a task allocate then release memory
- an independent task do the same later, and got allocated the same memory
    -> false-positive
#### How Taskgrind manages it
Overrides malloc/free - makes free perform a no-op



### Thread Local Storage (TLS)
#### Problem
1) Stack accesses - independent tasks scheduled on the same thread uses its stack
2) Standard TLS (using `__thread` or `pthread_setspecific()` ...)
We could probably detect it with ExTLS - kernel GDT table...
3) "Hand-crafted" TLS - can it be detected ???? probably not
```
for (int i = 0 ; i < 2 ; ++i)
    # pragma omp task
        write(x[omp_get_thread_id()]);
```
#### How Taskgrind manages it
2) and 3) - it doesn't
1) Ignore stack accesses above task's entry-point stack-frame
```c
# pragma omp task
{
    // no issues

    # pragma omp task // A
        int x = 0;

    # pragma omp task // B
        int x = 1;
}
```

```c
# pragma omp task
{
    // an issue

    int x;

    # pragma omp task // A
        x = 0;

    # pragma omp task // B
        x = 1;

    # pragma omp taskwait
}
```


### OpenMP limitations
#### Workshare
Cannot map parallel for chunk creation (from callback_work) to chunk scheduling (callback_dispatch)

### LLVM specific behaviors in single-thread
#### Workshare / Sections
Dispatch not raised with a single thread - https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/96698

#### Parallel region
No "sync" callback
```
# pragma omp parallel
{
} // no sync callback raised
```

# DETECTION ALGORITHM
- n = number of segments
- m = number of non-continuous accesses per segments
-> O(log(m) * n²)

TaskSanitizer - [21] is O(n²m²)

```bash
for each segment s1  # n
    for each segments s2 != s1 # n
        if s1 and s2 are concurrent, then
            if (s1.w n s2.w) u (s1.w n s2.r) u (s1.r n s2.w) is non-empty # log(m)
                error
```


# IDEAS
- Can specialize analysis to report errors more precisely (right now, only reports "determinacy race issue")
    - 2 siblings (use a dependence or taskwait)
    - 2 non-siblings (use taskwait)
    - a child task accesses its parent stack after its completion (use taskwait)
    - a task is reading uninitialized GPU memory (target tasks, read but no write previously)

- Can provide performance hints (not CORRECTNESS !!)
    - over-synchronized
        - 2 tasks are declared dependent but are not access-wide
        - 2 consecutive barriers
    - multiple-edges - out(x, y) -> in(x, y) - useless overheads, out(x) -> in(x) is sufficient
    - redundant-edges - out(x, x, ..., x)
    - arc redundant by transivity -
        - out(x, y) -> out(x) -> in(x)
               |_________________^
    - cache-use - a task accesses fits / does not fit into cache lines

- Task memory accesses mapping (see Numamma - https://github.com/numamma/numamma)


